CAT = "mlsHeme"; SEG = "HEM"
Q = [
('Hemoglobin is 10.5 g/dL and hematocrit is 35%. What is the MCHC?',
 '30.0 g/dL', ['3.0 g/dL', '33.3 g/dL', '36.0 g/dL'],
 'MCHC = Hgb / Hct x 100 = 10.5 / 35 x 100 = 30.0 g/dL, below the 32 to 36 range, so the cells are hypochromic. Dividing Hct by Hgb gives 33.3.'),
('Red cell fragments in a patient with burns can cause a falsely high:',
 'Impedance platelet count', ['Red cell count', 'Hemoglobin value', 'White cell count'],
 'Small fragments fall in the platelet size window of impedance counters. An optical or immunologic platelet count, or a smear estimate, resolves it.'),
('A reticulocyte count is 6.0% with a hematocrit of 27%. Using a normal hematocrit of 45%, what is the corrected reticulocyte count?',
 '3.6%', ['1.8%', '6.0%', '10.0%'],
 'Corrected = 6.0 x 27 / 45 = 3.6%. Turning the ratio over gives 10.0%; dividing again by a maturation time is the separate reticulocyte production index.'),
('Which flow cytometry test supports hereditary spherocytosis?',
 "Eosin-5'-maleimide dye binding", ['Loss of CD55 and CD59', 'FLAER binding to granulocytes', 'Fetal hemoglobin staining'],
 'EMA binds band 3, which is reduced in hereditary spherocytosis, so the cells fluoresce less. CD55, CD59 and FLAER are PNH tests.'),
('Round macrocytes with target cells and a normal vitamin B12 most suggest:',
 'Chronic liver disease', ['Pernicious anemia', 'Folate deficiency', 'Thalassemia minor'],
 'Extra cholesterol and phospholipid in red cell membranes give round macrocytes and target cells in liver disease. Megaloblastic anemia gives oval macrocytes.'),
('Blunt, finger-like crystals jutting from red cells, with many target cells, suggest:',
 'Hemoglobin SC disease', ['Hemoglobin CC disease', 'Hemoglobin SS disease', 'Sickle cell trait'],
 'SC cells form blunt, finger-like crystals with target cells. Hemoglobin CC disease gives hexagonal crystals, and SS disease shows classic sickle forms.'),
('Hemoglobins S and D migrate together on alkaline electrophoresis. Which method separates them?',
 'Acid citrate agar electrophoresis', ['Repeat alkaline cellulose acetate', 'Isopropanol stability test', 'Kleihauer-Betke acid elution'],
 'At acid pH, S moves away from the A and D position, so D is identified. Isopropanol detects unstable hemoglobins, not D.'),
('In the Kleihauer-Betke stain, fetal red cells stay pink because hemoglobin F:',
 'Resists acid elution', ['Binds more eosin', 'Resists alkali denaturation', 'Lacks heme groups'],
 'Adult hemoglobin washes out of fixed cells in acid buffer, leaving ghosts, while F stays and takes the counterstain. Alkali resistance is the basis of the Apt test.'),
('Blasts that are myeloperoxidase negative and TdT, CD10 and CD19 positive indicate:',
 'B-lymphoblastic leukemia', ['Acute myeloid leukemia', 'T-lymphoblastic leukemia', 'Burkitt lymphoma'],
 'TdT marks immature lymphoblasts, and CD19 with CD10 marks B lineage. Burkitt cells are mature B cells that are TdT negative.'),
('Which finding separates chronic myeloid leukemia from a leukemoid reaction?',
 'BCR::ABL1 fusion', ['High neutrophil count', 'Left shift to myelocytes', 'Toxic granulation'],
 'The Philadelphia chromosome t(9;22) makes the BCR::ABL1 fusion in CML. Both conditions raise neutrophils with a left shift, and toxic granulation favors a reaction to infection.'),
('Small mature lymphocytes that are CD5, CD19 and CD23 positive suggest:',
 'Chronic lymphocytic leukemia', ['Mantle cell lymphoma', 'Hairy cell leukemia', 'Follicular lymphoma'],
 'CLL cells co-express CD5 and CD23 on B cells. Mantle cell lymphoma is CD5 positive but CD23 negative, and follicular lymphoma is CD10 positive and CD5 negative.'),
('Macrocytic anemia, small hypolobated megakaryocytes and an isolated del(5q) suggest:',
 'Myelodysplastic neoplasm', ['Acute promyelocytic leukemia', 'Chronic myeloid leukemia', 'Primary myelofibrosis'],
 'MDS with isolated del(5q) causes macrocytic anemia, often with normal or high platelets and hypolobated megakaryocytes. It is defined by the cytogenetic finding.'),
('Platelets persistently above 450 x 10^3/µL with a CALR mutation suggest:',
 'Essential thrombocythemia', ['Reactive thrombocytosis', 'Polycythemia vera', 'Iron deficiency anemia'],
 'CALR, JAK2 and MPL are the driver mutations of essential thrombocythemia. Reactive thrombocytosis from infection or iron deficiency has no driver mutation.'),
('Circulating CD4-positive T cells with folded, brain-like nuclei and red skin suggest:',
 'Sezary syndrome', ['Hairy cell leukemia', 'Adult T-cell leukemia', 'Prolymphocytic leukemia'],
 'Sezary cells are cerebriform CD4 T cells of a leukemic cutaneous T-cell lymphoma. Adult T-cell leukemia has flower-shaped nuclei.'),
('Giant fused granules in neutrophils with partial albinism and recurrent infections suggest:',
 'Chediak-Higashi syndrome', ['Alder-Reilly anomaly', 'May-Hegglin anomaly', 'Hereditary myeloperoxidase lack'],
 'A defect in lysosomal trafficking fuses granules into giant ones and impairs killing. Alder-Reilly granules are dark but normal-sized and do not affect function.'),
('Forty-five white cells are counted in all nine large squares of a Neubauer chamber, with undiluted CSF. What is the count?',
 '50 cells/µL', ['5 cells/µL', '45 cells/µL', '450 cells/µL'],
 'Nine 1 mm2 squares at 0.1 mm depth hold 0.9 µL, so 45 / 0.9 = 50 cells/µL. Reporting the raw count ignores the volume counted.'),
('WBC 5.0 x 10^3/µL, lymphocytes 30%, and CD4 cells 20% of lymphocytes. What is the absolute CD4 count?',
 '300 cells/µL', ['150 cells/µL', '1,000 cells/µL', '1,500 cells/µL'],
 '5,000 x 0.30 x 0.20 = 300 cells/µL. Taking 20% of the whole WBC gives 1,000, and 1,500 is the absolute lymphocyte count.'),
('Pappenheimer bodies that stain with Prussian blue contain:',
 'Iron', ['DNA', 'RNA', 'Hemoglobin'],
 'Pappenheimer bodies are iron-containing granules, seen in sideroblastic anemia and after splenectomy. Howell-Jolly bodies are DNA and basophilic stippling is RNA.'),
('Heinz bodies are shown best with which stain?',
 'Crystal violet', ['Wright-Giemsa', 'Prussian blue', 'Sudan black B stain'],
 'Denatured hemoglobin precipitates are seen only with a supravital stain such as crystal violet. Wright stain does not show them, and Prussian blue stains iron.'),
('Acute myeloid leukemia with abnormal eosinophils in the marrow is linked with:',
 'inv(16)', ['t(8;21)', 't(15;17)', 't(9;22)'],
 'inv(16) or t(16;16), CBFB::MYH11, gives AML with abnormal eosinophils. t(8;21) gives AML with maturation, and t(15;17) is promyelocytic leukemia.'),
('A rapidly growing lymphoma with a starry-sky pattern and t(8;14) is:',
 'Burkitt lymphoma', ['Mantle cell lymphoma', 'Follicular lymphoma', 'Hodgkin lymphoma'],
 't(8;14) puts MYC next to the immunoglobulin heavy chain locus. Mantle cell lymphoma has t(11;14), and follicular lymphoma has t(14;18).'),
('A myeloid-to-erythroid ratio of 1:1 in a patient with hemolytic anemia reflects:',
 'Erythroid hyperplasia', ['Myeloid hypoplasia from drugs', 'Normal adult marrow', 'Myeloid leukemia'],
 'The normal adult M:E ratio is about 3:1. Brisk hemolysis expands red cell precursors, lowering the ratio.'),
('Which result pattern supports hemolysis?',
 'Low haptoglobin, high LD', ['High haptoglobin, low LD', 'High haptoglobin, high LD', 'Low haptoglobin, low LD'],
 'Free hemoglobin binds and clears haptoglobin, and red cells release LD. Haptoglobin is an acute phase protein, so inflammation can mask a fall.'),
('Golf-ball inclusions after incubation with brilliant cresyl blue indicate:',
 'Hemoglobin H disease', ['Beta thalassemia major', 'Lead poisoning', 'Unstable hemoglobin Koln'],
 'Hemoglobin H (beta-4 tetramers) precipitates with the dye into many small dots. It forms when three of four alpha genes are lost.'),
]
